{# What `{{login}}` renders when nobody asked for anything else: the account button.

   Signed out it is an icon, signed in it is your face -- one control, in the corner of
   every page, that says whether the wiki knows who you are and takes you to /user either
   way. What it is NOT is a form: `@core/login-form.twig` is that, and a page dedicated to
   signing in asks for it by name. A whole set of fields inlined into the navigation bar of
   every page, for the one visitor in a hundred who is about to use it, is what this
   replaced.

   No visible label, so the button is the width of the disc wherever it lands; the name it
   would have shown is on the link instead, where a screen reader and a hover both find it.

   Signed out, the link carries the page it was clicked from in `?return=`, so signing in
   ends where it started rather than on the account screen. LoginAction builds that URL --
   whether a return is worth carrying is a question about the current request, not about
   markup (see getAccountUrl()).

   Still the `{{login}}` action rather than a plain link in the squelette, because only the
   action knows whether there is a session, whose name to show and which face to draw. #}
{% if class is empty %}
    {% block main %}
        {% set label = connected ? _t('LOGIN_CONNECTED_AS') ~ ' ' ~ user : _t('LOGIN_LOGIN') %}
        <a class="navbar-btn account-link {{ not nobtn ? 'yw-btn ' ~ btnclass : '' }}"
           href="{{ connected ? url({ tag: 'user' }) : accounturl }}"
           data-tooltip="tooltip"
           data-placement="bottom"
           title="{{ label }}"
           aria-label="{{ label }}">
            {%- if connected -%}
                {% include '@core/_avatar.twig' with { avatar: avatar } only %}
            {%- else -%}
                {{ icon('user') }}
            {%- endif -%}
        </a>
    {% endblock %}
{% else %}
    <div class="{{ class }}">{{ block("main") }}</div>
{% endif %}
